            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Life in Arles</strong><br>\nSeeking inspiration and the vibrant light of the south, van Gogh moved to Arles, France, in 1888. Here, he produced some of his most famous works, including \"The Yellow House,\" \"The Bedroom,\" and \"Sunflowers.\" His hope was to establish an artists' colony in Arles, but this dream was never realized. During his time in Arles, van Gogh's mental health began to deteriorate, leading to the infamous incident where he cut off part of his ear.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Starry Night</strong><br>\nThe Starry Night is one of Vincent van Gogh's most iconic and celebrated works. Painted in 1889 while he was in an asylum in Saint-Rémy-de-Provence, this piece captures the view from his window, although it was painted from memory during the day. Its swirling skies, luminous stars, and illuminated windows exemplify van Gogh's imaginative style and use of color to convey emotion.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Relationship with Theo</strong><br>\nVincent had a very close and affectionate relationship with his younger brother Theo, who provided him with financial support and unwavering encouragement throughout his career. The brothers' letters reveal a profound bond, with Theo often providing advice and critique on Vincent's work. Unfortunately, this constant support also created a dependency that affected Vincent's confidence in his abilities and his financial autonomy.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
