          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Mental Health Struggles</strong><br>\nVan Gogh struggled with mental health issues for most of his adult life, which often impacted his ability to work and maintain relationships. He suffered from psychotic episodes and delusions, which led to his hospitalization on several occasions. Despite his struggles, he continued to paint and produced a vast body of work, totaling over 2,100 artworks, including around 860 oil paintings, in just over a decade.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy and Death</strong><br>\nVincent van Gogh died on July 29, 1890, from a gunshot wound, under circumstances that remain unclear; it is commonly believed to be a suicide. His work received little recognition during his lifetime, but he posthumously became one of the most famous and influential figures in the history of Western art. Today, van Gogh's paintings are admired for their beauty, emotion, and color and fetch some of the highest prices at art auctions around the world.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Posthumous Fame</strong><br>\nFollowing his death, van Gogh's fame grew due to his sister-in-law's efforts to promote his art. Exhibitions of his work in Paris and Amsterdam brought him to public attention, and by the mid-20th century, he was seen as a misunderstood genius. His life and work have inspired countless artists, making him a pivotal figure in the art world, whose popularity continues to endure over a century later.</p>\n"
              },
